      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\), we start by examining the expression more closely. Let's denote \\( y = \\arctan x \\). Then the function becomes:\n\n\\[ f(x) = y + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\]\n\nNext, we need to simplify the expression \\( \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\). Notice that the expression \\( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\) is the tangent of \\( -\\arctan x \\) because:\n\n\\[ \\tan \\left( -\\arctan x \\right) = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\]\n\nSo, we can rewrite the function as:\n\n\\[ f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\left( -\\arctan x \\right) \\]\n\nUsing the identity for the sum of arctangents, we know that:\n\n\\[ \\arctan a + \\arctan b = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{a + b}{1 - ab} \\right) \\]\n\nprovided that \\( ab < 1 \\). Here, \\( a = x \\) and \\( b = -\\arctan x \\). Therefore,\n\n\\[ \\arctan x + \\arctan \\left( -\\arctan x \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x + (-\\arctan x)}{1 - x(-\\arctan x)} \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x - \\arctan x}{1 + x \\arctan x} \\right) \\]\n\nIf we let \\( t = \\arctan x \\), then \\( \\arctan x = t \\), and the expression becomes:\n\n\\[ \\frac{t - t}{1 + t t} = \\frac{0}{1 + t^2} = 0 \\]\n\nSo, the function simplifies to:\n\n\\[ f(x) = \\arctan t \\]\n\nSince \\( t = \\arctan x \\) and the range of \\( \\arctan x \\) is \\( \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\), the range of \\( f(x) \\) is also \\( \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\]",
