      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\), we will analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's use the identity for the arctangent of a sum:\n\\[ \\arctan a + \\arctan b = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{a + b}{1 - ab} \\right) \\]\nprovided that \\( ab < 1 \\).\n\nHere, \\( a = x \\) and \\( b = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\). Let's compute \\( ab \\):\n\\[ ab = x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x}. \\]\nFor \\( ab \\) to be less than 1, we need:\n\\[ \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x} < 1. \\]\nThis inequality can be solved by first assuming \\( x \\neq -1 \\):\n\\[ x - x^2 < 1 + x, \\]\n\\[ -x^2 < 1, \\]\n\\[ x^2 > -1. \\]\nSince \\( x^2 \\) is always non-negative, \\( x^2 > -1 \\) is always true. Therefore, the inequality \\( \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x} < 1 \\) is always true for all \\( x \\neq -1 \\).\n\nNow, we can use the arctangent identity:\n\\[ f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x + \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}}{1 - x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}} \\right). \\]\nSimplify the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[ x + \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{x(1 + x) + (1 - x)}{1 + x} = \\frac{x + x^2 + 1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + x}, \\]\n\\[ 1 - x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{(1 + x)(1 - x)}{1 + x} = 1 - x. \\]\nThus,\n\\[ f(x) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{\\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + x}}{1 - x} \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + x - x(1 + x)} \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + x - x - x^2} \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2} \\right). \\]\nSimplify further:\n\\[ f(x) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2} \\right). \\]\nSince the range of \\( \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2} \\right) \\) is \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) for all \\( x \\neq -1 \\), the final answer is:\n\\[ \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2} \\right). \\]\nTo express it in terms of a specific value, we can note that:\n\\[ \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2} \\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\arctan x. \\]\nBut since the range is \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right), the specific value we are looking for in the context of the range is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}. \\]",
